WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:04.890
In this video, we're talking about the commutative property, which is one of the properties of algebra.

00:04.890 --> 00:10.950
And basically the commutative property tells us that A plus B, for example, is exactly the same thing

00:10.950 --> 00:17.670
as B plus A, so this is going to be equal to B plus A that there's no difference here.

00:17.670 --> 00:22.980
You can remember this property as the commutative property because think about somebody who lives at

00:22.980 --> 00:23.670
home.

00:23.670 --> 00:31.320
They live in their house at home and they get in their car and they commute to their job.

00:31.350 --> 00:33.540
This is where they work.

00:33.750 --> 00:35.220
This is their office building.

00:35.220 --> 00:36.900
And every day they have their commute.

00:36.900 --> 00:41.160
They commute from home to work and then they commute back again.

00:41.160 --> 00:46.890
Whether they start from home and drive to work or start from work and drive to home, the distance between

00:46.890 --> 00:48.390
the two is the same.

00:48.390 --> 00:49.320
That doesn't change.

00:49.320 --> 00:54.300
So just because you flip the order doesn't mean you change the distance that you're actually driving.

00:54.300 --> 00:56.190
And this is kind of the same thing, right?

00:56.190 --> 01:02.220
We have a plus B, even if we flip the order to B plus A, the sum is still going to be the same thing.

01:02.220 --> 01:08.130
So if we apply this to real numbers and we say two plus three is equal to five, we know that three

01:08.130 --> 01:10.830
plus two is also equal to five.

01:10.830 --> 01:15.810
It doesn't matter which order we add these numbers together in, the result is still the same.

01:15.810 --> 01:20.400
Just because you've commuted these values to a different location, just because you've swapped the

01:20.400 --> 01:25.500
location and put the two where the three is and the three where the two is, the sum is still the same.

01:25.500 --> 01:29.520
This is going to work for addition, and it's also going to work for multiplication.

01:29.520 --> 01:38.430
So when we have X times Y, which we can also write as x y or as x y, these are all different ways

01:38.430 --> 01:39.660
to write multiplication.

01:39.660 --> 01:43.410
X times y is going to be equal to y, times x.

01:43.410 --> 01:48.420
It doesn't matter which order we multiply them together in, the product is still going to be the same.

01:48.420 --> 01:52.080
And if we apply this to real numbers, we see that that's true.

01:52.110 --> 01:55.440
We know four times six is equal to 24.

01:55.770 --> 02:00.480
If we compute the values and we switch the order, we put the six where the four is and the four where

02:00.510 --> 02:06.090
the six is, and we say six times four, we're still going to get 24.

02:06.120 --> 02:08.010
The result is still the same.

02:08.010 --> 02:12.420
The important thing to remember about the commutative property is that while it works for addition and

02:12.420 --> 02:18.030
it works for multiplication, it does not necessarily work for subtraction or for division.

02:18.030 --> 02:25.530
So for example, with subtraction, if we say six minus four is equal to positive two, if we flip the

02:25.530 --> 02:30.270
order and we say four minus six, that's going to be equal to negative two.

02:30.300 --> 02:31.650
Those are not the same thing.

02:31.650 --> 02:34.500
So it doesn't necessarily work for subtraction.

02:34.500 --> 02:40.320
If we do division and we say six divided by three, we get positive two.

02:40.320 --> 02:46.080
If we flip the order and we say three divided by six, we get one half, not two.

02:46.080 --> 02:47.040
So these aren't equal.

02:47.040 --> 02:51.570
And we can say that the commutative property doesn't necessarily work for subtraction and division.

02:51.570 --> 02:57.090
So just remember the commutative property for addition and for multiplication tells you that the order

02:57.090 --> 02:59.970
of addition and the order of multiplication doesn't matter.

02:59.970 --> 03:01.800
You're still going to get the same result.
